-- ============================================================================
-- PD9 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Production Surveillance, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: WHAT A NUMBER IS WORTH ONCE YOU KNOW WHICH COLUMN IT
-- CAME FROM. Every one of its six graded fields is the SAME data read a second
-- way: a period ratio formed as a mean of daily ratios and the same ratio
-- formed volumetrically, an allocation run with the failed tests out and the
-- same allocation with them in, a decline read off a fit whose exponent
-- decided nothing, and a rate read as oil and the same rate read as liquid.
-- NOTHING NEW IS INTRODUCED IN THIS TIER AT ALL. That is deliberate and it is
-- the whole shape of the argument: the tier is the Associate and Professional
-- calls re-run under a different reading of the identical rows, and if one new
-- function were needed to reach these findings they would be a different
-- module's problem instead of this one's.
--
-- THE ORACLES RECORD ALMOST NONE OF IT, AND THAT IS THE STARTING POINT. All
-- four are genuinely independent referees and the course says so before it
-- says anything critical. The surveillance oracle does every date arithmetic
-- on the calendar where the module counts epoch-millisecond day numbers, forms
-- every window mean by explicit calendar membership from the STATED window
-- definition rather than the implemented inequality, and measures effective
-- decline through the Arps rate law where the module evaluates a closed form.
-- The allocation oracle splits the metered total as a SHARE where the module
-- multiplies by a precomputed factor, and bisects into explicit validity
-- intervals where the module scans forward and breaks. The screening oracle
-- re-expresses every rule as a declarative penalty ledger walked by one
-- generic scorer with no branch on a method anywhere. The advisor oracle takes
-- the reference stage as a covering SET and the reconciliation as a full
-- four-way truth table. They gate what each function RETURNS on the inputs the
-- goldens publish. They do not gate WHICH OF TWO FUNCTIONS A CALLER SHOULD
-- HAVE USED, what a missing column means, or what a refusal sentence says, and
-- that is where this entire tier lives. Two of the seams ARE published, as
-- MEASURED DISAGREEMENTS rather than as expected values, in
-- `surveillance_cases.ratioSeam` and `lift_screening_cases.seams`. Publishing
-- a disagreement instead of resolving it is the right thing to have done and
-- the tier credits it wherever it quotes them.
--
-- THE FIRST FINDING, AND IT IS THE ONE THE TIER IS NAMED FOR. ONE RATIO, READ
-- TWO WAYS, BY TWO FUNCTIONS IN ONE FILE. `computeKpis` forms a period
-- watercut and gas-oil ratio VOLUMETRICALLY, sum over sum, which is what a
-- period ratio means. `detectExceptions` forms them as the MEAN OF THE DAILY
-- RATIOS, which is a different quantity and is biased by low-rate days. The
-- module's header states it and the golden PUBLISHES the size of it on P-1:
-- a gas-oil ratio rise of 70.033482142857 per cent read one way against
-- 42.737789203085 read the other, an overstatement of 19.122961825433 PER CENT
-- on the published case, and that gap is the difference between a printed HIGH
-- and a printed MEDIUM. On the teaching well the same seam is far larger,
-- because the recent window mixes four ordinary days with three collapsed
-- ones: the gas-oil ratio rise is 83.907484614181 per cent one way and
-- 11.250129499613 per cent the other, which is a HIGH exception against NO
-- EXCEPTION AT ALL, and the watercut rise is 21.474523054592 points against
-- 7.555079897248. NEITHER READING IS WRONG. A mean of daily ratios answers
-- "what did a typical day look like" and a ratio of sums answers "what did the
-- period produce". The defect is that one module uses both, on the same rows,
-- and says so only in a source comment. And the sweep shows why nobody caught
-- it: over a window of UNIFORM days the two readings are IDENTICAL, at zero of
-- seven collapsed days and again at seven of seven, and they diverge only in
-- the middle, peaking at a ratio of 1.803701012112. A test fixture of tidy
-- days cannot show this at all.
--
-- THE SECOND FINDING, AND IT IS THE LARGEST. THE FOUR PRODUCING-DAY COLUMNS
-- ARE READ BY EXACTLY ONE FUNCTION IN THE FILE, AND IT IS NOT THE ONE DECIDING
-- WHICH WELLS CHANGED. `derivePoint` computes `oilPd`, `waterPd`, `gasPd` and
-- `liquidPd` on every row of every well. Grep the module: they are read in one
-- place, `rateSeriesForFit`, which is the DECLINE OVERLAY. `detectExceptions`,
-- whose entire job is to say which wells have changed, picks its column with
-- one line, `const rateKey = isInjector ? 'winj' : 'oil'`, and `oil` is the
-- CALENDAR VOLUME booked against the row. So the function that decides where
-- an engineer goes today reads the column that moves when a well is simply
-- DOWN, and never reads the column that says how the well is performing. On
-- OGUTA-6 the producing-day oil rate is 512.000000000 stb/d on all seven
-- recent days while the calendar volume falls from 502.666666666667 stb to
-- 307.504761904762 stb, and the engine reports a drop of 38.825312618416 per
-- cent on a well whose deliverability went UP by 1.856763925729 per cent. THE
-- EXCEPTION THAT WOULD HAVE NAMED THE CAUSE DOES NOT FIRE EITHER: the mean
-- recent hours are 14.414285714286 against a `downtimeHours` threshold of 12.
-- A surveillance report that names an effect and suppresses its cause is worse
-- than one that names neither. And the message hard-codes the unit it is
-- wrong about: `stb/d` is printed after a mean of calendar VOLUMES on every
-- ledger, not only on the coarse one where it is obvious.
--
-- THE THIRD FINDING. A MISSING VALUE HAS FOUR SPELLINGS AND FOUR CONVENTIONS
-- ACROSS THE FOUR MODULES, AND THEY CONTRADICT EACH OTHER ON THE SAME COLUMN.
--
--   ONE. `derivePoint` coerces a volume with `row.oil_stb || 0`, which returns
--   the STRING when the column arrived as text. Every derived quantity formed
--   by multiplication or division coerces back to a number and is exactly
--   right; the one formed by ADDITION is `liquid`, and plus on two strings
--   concatenates. So on one row of 800 stb of oil, 200 stb of water and 400
--   Mscf of gas the gas-oil ratio and the producing-day oil rate are correct
--   and the watercut is understated and the liquid producing-day rate
--   overstated by the same factor of 800.2. `buildFieldSeries` accumulates
--   onto the same string, so four identical rows of 800 stb give a field total
--   of 800800800800. Nothing anywhere reports that a column arrived as text.
--
--   TWO. THE SAME ABSENT HOURS COLUMN IS UPTIME UNKNOWN IN SURVEILLANCE AND
--   TWENTY-FOUR HOURS ON IN ALLOCATION. `derivePoint` leaves the volume
--   unscaled and makes no claim; `computeAllocation` reads
--   `Number.isFinite(row?.hours_on) ? row.hours_on : defaultHours` with
--   `defaultHours` at 24, and when there is no ledger row AT ALL the lookup is
--   null, the guard is false, and the well is credited with a full day on
--   stream. So a well that filed nothing takes a FULL SHARE of the metered
--   total. Two wells with identical tests and a metered total of 1000 stb: the
--   one that was shut in and SAID SO takes nothing, and the one that was shut
--   in and filed NO ROW takes half. The only thing that changed is which rows
--   exist. An unscaled volume and a full day happen to be the same NUMBER and
--   are not the same CLAIM, and the two functions are called on the same
--   ledger by the same studio.
--
--   THREE. `!b` IS TRUE FOR NaN, SO A BROKEN EXPONENT BECOMES AN EXPONENTIAL.
--   `annualEffectiveDecline` reads `if (modelType === 'Exponential' || !b)`,
--   and `!NaN` is true, and so is `!null` and `!undefined`. At Di 0.0015 per
--   day with modelType Hyperbolic, b = 0.5 returns 38.364403131474 per cent
--   and b = NaN returns 42.160601062199. THAT SECOND NUMBER IS CASE 1 OF THE
--   PUBLISHED GOLDEN. A hyperbolic fit whose exponent came back unusable
--   returns a number a reviewer has seen before and will not question, rather
--   than a refusal or a NaN, and there is nothing in the return to distrust.
--   A negative b is not refused either: b = -0.5 raises a NEGATIVE bracket,
--   -0.825, to a power and comes back with an ordinary 31.9375 per cent. Di,
--   by contrast, IS guarded properly and returns null for zero, for negative,
--   for NaN and for null, so the module knows how to write this guard and
--   wrote it correctly one line earlier.
--
--   FOUR. `screenLift` READS MISSING NUMBERS AS THE WORST CASE AND MISSING
--   BOOLEANS AS THE BEST, AND THE SAME QUANTITY IS COERCED TWO OPPOSITE WAYS
--   TWO FILES APART. `screenLift({})` on an empty object scores the rod pump
--   at 100 and RECOMMENDS it, because depth zero times rate zero is the most
--   favourable duty index the rule has, and the two sixty-point deductions in
--   the matrix are the two that silence can never trigger. Told in addition
--   that there is no power and no gas at the site, which is strictly MORE
--   information and all of it bad, the rod pump still scores 100. An absent
--   API is ZERO in `screenLift`, heavier than any real crude; an absent API is
--   32 degrees in `liftAdvisor.liquidGravity`, which uses `num(api, 32)`, and
--   a STATED zero there is taken literally and gives a specific gravity of
--   1.076045627376, denser than water. Hand a model with no fluid description
--   to the two halves of the advisor and one says light oil and the other says
--   the heaviest crude on earth.
--
-- THE FOURTH FINDING. THE GUARDS SIT ON THE WRONG QUANTITY.
--
--   `minOilRate` GATES TWO CHECKS OF THREE AND NOT THE ONE THAT FIRES. It
--   gates the rate check and the gas-oil ratio check and does NOT gate the
--   watercut check, so a well at 3.0 stb/d in the baseline and 1.2 recently is
--   too small to have a SIXTY PER CENT rate collapse reported and still raises
--   a HIGH watercut exception on the same rows. The exception list is
--   therefore not monotonic in `minOilRate`.
--
--   THE DOWNTIME GUARD CANNOT REPORT THE WORST POSSIBLE UPTIME. The branch is
--   `hrs.count && hrs.mean < s.downtimeHours && hrs.mean > 0`. A well
--   averaging 0.10 hours on stream raises a medium; a well averaging EXACTLY
--   0.00 hours raises nothing. The one uptime value that is unambiguously
--   worst is the one value the check will not report. The clause was
--   presumably written to exclude an absent hours column and is not needed for
--   that, because `mean()` already filters non-finite values and `hrs.count`
--   already guards the empty case one condition earlier. And `shut_in` does
--   not cover it: `shut_in` fires on a recent RATE of zero, and a well that
--   books volumes against a zero hours column has a positive rate, so it gets
--   no shut_in and now no downtime either.
--
--   `maxTestAgeDays` = 0 TURNS THE AGE CHECK OFF. `testInForce` guards with
--   `Number.isFinite(x) && x > 0`, so zero falls through to no check at all
--   and a test 2088 days old carries its well. An operator typing zero to mean
--   "no test may be stale" gets the opposite. On the teaching field the sweep
--   runs monotonically down to one day, where a single well carries a
--   theoretical of 2072.000000000 stb, and then at ZERO days it JUMPS BACK to
--   four wells and 65033.920833333 stb, which is the same answer the loosest
--   setting on the dial gives. The strictest-looking setting is the loosest
--   behaviour in the module.
--
--   AND A CLAUSE THAT CAN NEVER BE TRUE. The gas-oil ratio gate carries
--   `(base.mean == null || base.mean >= s.minOilRate)`. `derivePoint` sets
--   `oil` with `row.oil_stb || 0`, so oil is ALWAYS a finite number on every
--   point, and `base` is the mean of oil over the same window, so `base.mean`
--   is null only when the window holds no points, in which case
--   `gorBase.count` is zero and the gate has already shut two clauses earlier.
--   The escape hatch is unreachable, and the digest proves it by construction
--   rather than by argument.
--
-- AND THE SAME SHAPE AT THE FAR END OF THE COURSE, IN A DIFFERENT MODULE.
-- `liftScreening` documents its `targetRate` as bbl/d of LIQUID and
-- `liftAdvisor` uses the identical input as the OIL design rate, and the
-- shipped studio passes ONE number to both. Both headers say so and hold the
-- resolution as an owner decision, which is why this is quoted as a STATED
-- seam. The golden publishes it on one well: 300 stb/d of oil at a 70 per cent
-- water cut is 999.9999999999999 bbl/d of liquid, and the rod pump moves from
-- 80 to 40 while the recommended set does not change. On the teaching well at
-- 9200 ft, swept across the bands the rules actually use, FOUR of the six
-- methods move, and the largest single move is plunger lift losing 45 points,
-- which removes it from consideration entirely, with not one datum about the
-- well changing.
--
-- ONE FUNCTION IN THIS DOMAIN READS THE WALL CLOCK, AND THE TIER HAS TO SAY SO
-- WITHOUT EVER QUOTING WHAT IT RETURNS. `summarizeDeferments` defaults its
-- `asOf` to today, in a module whose header says every window anchors on the
-- field's latest ledger date precisely so an old dataset surveils honestly. It
-- is the only function in `surveillance.js` that can give a different answer
-- tomorrow on the same data, the difference is exactly one day per elapsed
-- day, and nothing in the return says which anchor was used. Its day count is
-- also clamped with `Math.max(1, ...)`, so an event whose end date is a MONTH
-- BEFORE its start is silently one day, exactly like an event that started and
-- ended on the same day. Everything this course says about it is stated as a
-- BOOLEAN or as an ANCHORED recomputation and never as a wall-clock number,
-- because a figure nobody can reproduce is not a figure anybody should act on,
-- and a digest that changes on every rebuild cannot be gated.
--
-- WHAT THE TIER ENDS KNOWING, AND IT IS ONE SENTENCE. A SURVEILLANCE NUMBER IS
-- A READING, AND THE READING WAS CHOSEN BY WHOEVER WROTE THE CALL. Which
-- column, over which window, by which of the several functions that form the
-- same quantity differently. None of that makes these modules useless. It
-- makes every number they return conditional on three choices, and naming the
-- three beside the number is the whole of what an expert reading is.
-- ============================================================================

insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
values ('surveillance', 'advanced', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-one-ratio-read-two-ways","title":"One Ratio Read Two Ways","lesson_keys":["l01-a-mean-of-ratios-and-a-ratio-of-sums","l02-where-the-two-agree","l03-what-makes-them-diverge","l04-a-severity-that-changes-with-the-reading","l05-neither-reading-is-wrong"]},{"key":"m02-the-column-that-is-never-read","title":"The Column That Is Never Read","lesson_keys":["l01-computed-on-every-row","l02-the-rate-key-the-engine-picks","l03-a-well-that-did-not-change","l04-the-exception-that-would-have-explained-it","l05-what-a-period-row-does-not-say"]},{"key":"m03-the-spellings-of-nothing","title":"The Spellings of Nothing","lesson_keys":["l01-four-modules-four-conventions","l02-a-number-that-arrived-as-text","l03-an-absent-row-and-a-full-share","l04-a-missing-exponent-read-as-zero","l05-what-a-default-decides"]},{"key":"m04-guards-in-the-wrong-place","title":"Guards in the Wrong Place","lesson_keys":["l01-a-gate-on-two-checks-of-three","l02-a-clause-that-can-never-fire","l03-a-limit-that-turns-itself-off","l04-a-refusal-that-names-another-number","l05-a-guard-that-passes-what-it-cannot-read"]},{"key":"m05-one-rate-two-phases","title":"One Rate, Two Phases","lesson_keys":["l01-the-handoff-between-two-halves","l02-where-the-screening-scores-move","l03-a-checklist-is-not-a-calculation"]},{"key":"m06-the-expert-reading","title":"The Expert Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
on conflict (app_slug, tier) do update
  set structure = excluded.structure,
      content_version = excluded.content_version,
      active = true,
      updated_at = now();

delete from public.academy_quiz_questions
 where app_slug = 'surveillance' and tier = 'advanced';

insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 1, 'Which function in surveillance.js forms a period gas-oil ratio as the unweighted mean of the daily ratios?', '["computeKpis, which takes the mean of the field oil and the mean of the field gas over its window and only then divides the one by the other.", "detectExceptions, which calls windowMean on the gor key over ratios derivePoint already formed row by row.", "buildFieldSeries, which adds every row on a date together before dividing the field gas by the field oil for that day.", "derivePoint, which forms the ratio on one row and hands the window a quantity already weighted by that row''s volume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in that mean is weighted by volume, so a collapsed day speaks for a seventh of a 7 day window, while computeKpis is volume weighted by construction.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 2, 'The published seam on P-1 gives a recent gas-oil ratio of 1360.267857142857 scf/stb one way and 1141.902313624679 scf/stb the other. Which of the two can be multiplied by the days in the window to recover the gas the well actually made?', '["1360.267857142857 scf/stb, the mean of daily ratios, because averaging the ratio day by day is what a period ratio means.", "Either of them, since the golden records the gap between the two as 19.122961825433 per cent and calls neither of them an error.", "1141.902313624679 scf/stb, the volumetric reading, because it is the window''s gas over the window''s oil.", "Neither, because an exception row carrying a gas-oil ratio prints no window length beside it to multiply by."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only a ratio of sums returns a volume when it is multiplied out. The mean of daily ratios answers what a typical day looked like.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 3, 'What quantity does surveillance_cases.ratioSeam publish as 19.122961825433 per cent?', '["The overstatement of the gas-oil ratio rise read as a mean of daily ratios against the same rise read volumetrically.", "The gas-oil ratio rise on the recent window itself, which is the value the gor_rise exception row prints beside a baseline of 800.000000000000 scf/stb.", "The tolerance the gate allows between the two readings before the ratioSeam case is failed.", "The watercut rise, expressed against the baseline watercut rather than in points."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two rises are 70.033482142857 per cent and 42.737789203085 per cent, and the golden commits the size of the gap instead of picking a winner.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 4, 'Over the published baseline window both readings of the gas-oil ratio return 800.000000000000 scf/stb. What does that agreement establish?', '["That detectExceptions and computeKpis hold the same formula, which the recent window then contradicts, so one of the two must form its ratio differently on a fallen rate.", "That the baseline window was long enough for the volume weighting inside computeKpis to average itself away.", "That the well held a steady oil rate through the baseline, since weighting can only bite where the rate moves.", "Nothing about the two formulas, because a window whose daily ratios do not move gives a mean of ratios and a ratio of sums the same answer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Four consecutive teaching baseline days book 1042.000000, 1019.000000, 1063.000000 and 1031.000000 stb of oil at one gas-oil ratio of 580.000000000 scf/stb, so the rate moved and the ratio did not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 5, 'The teaching seam sweep collapses k of 7 recent days. The two readings are identical at 0 collapsed and identical again at 7 collapsed. Why does the agreement come back at the far end?', '["Seven collapsed days are seven days of one shape, and a window holding one day shape gives volume weighting nothing to move.", "The gas-oil ratio at 7 of 7 is 1719.512195121951 scf/stb, large enough that the difference between the readings is lost in rounding.", "At a total collapse the volumetric reading falls back on the mean of daily ratios, because the window sum is formed from one row.", "The watercut difference is 0.000000000000 at that end, and both gas-oil ratio readings are formed off that same watercut."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ratio column reads 1.000000000000 at both ends of the sweep and peaks at 1.803701012112 at 5 of 7, so the disagreement is a mixture effect.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 6, 'The gas-oil ratio ladder is a trigger of 30 per cent with a doubling to high at 60 per cent. On the published seam well, which reading crosses the doubling?', '["Both readings, since 42.737789203085 per cent is already past the trigger and the trigger is the threshold that names a high.", "Neither reading, because the doubling is applied to the 19.122961825433 per cent gap between the two rather than to either one.", "The mean of daily ratios at 70.033482142857 per cent, while the volumetric reading at 42.737789203085 per cent prints medium.", "The volumetric reading, since a ratio of sums is always the larger of the two wherever a window mixes rates."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One reading sits above 60 per cent and the other below it, so the same rows print two different severities on the same ladder.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 7, 'On the teaching seam well the gas-oil ratio rise is 83.907484614181 per cent read as a mean of daily ratios and 11.250129499613 per cent read volumetrically. What does the volumetric reading put on the exception list?', '["A medium gor_rise, since the rise is positive and any positive rise reaches the first rung of the ladder.", "Nothing at all, because 11.250129499613 per cent does not reach the 30 per cent trigger.", "A high gor_rise, because the severity is decided on the value of 1066.663410762250 scf/stb rather than on the size of the rise.", "An info gor_rise, which is the rank the ladder gives a rise that crosses no threshold at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One reading is a high exception and the other is not an exception, on one set of 7 rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 8, 'The teaching seam well''s recent watercut disagreement is printed as 0.139194431573 and again as 13.919443157344. What is the relation between the two?', '["The first is the difference over the recent window and the second is the difference over the baseline window.", "The first is the volumetric reading of the watercut and the second is the rise that reading implies against the baseline.", "The first is a difference between readings and the second is the per cent by which one reading exceeds the other.", "The first is the difference expressed as a fraction and the second is the same difference in points, a hundred apart."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The recent watercut is 0.451386451920 as a mean of daily ratios against 0.312192020347 volumetrically, and a watercut is a fraction in surveillance.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 9, 'The watercut ladder is 10 points to medium and 20 points to high. The teaching seam well rises 21.474523054592 points on one reading and 7.555079897248 points on the other. What do the two print?', '["A high watercut_rise against a medium watercut_rise, since the second reading still clears the 10 point trigger.", "A high watercut_rise against no exception of any kind.", "A medium watercut_rise against no exception, because a doubling needs the rise to exceed 20 points twice over.", "Two high watercut_rise rows, because the severity is taken off the recent watercut of 0.451386451920 rather than off the rise."]'::jsonb, 1, '7.555079897248 points is below the 10 point trigger, so the volumetric reading of those same rows never reaches the list.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 10, 'Which field in an exception row or a computeKpis return names the reading that formed the number in it?', '["The type field on the exception row, since gor_rise is raised only off the mean of daily ratios and never off the volumetric figure that computeKpis holds.", "The baseline field, which carries the volumetric figure beside the value so the two readings can be compared.", "No field in either, since one returns a type, a severity, a value and a baseline and the other returns watercut and gor under those names alone.", "The unit suffix on the returned watercut, a fraction for the volumetric reading and points for the mean of daily ratios."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine header states the seam and a header is not on the screen, so a watercut of 0.451386451920 arrives with no way to tell which function made it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 11, 'The teaching sweep at 3 of 7 collapsed reads 1068.566450970632 scf/stb as a mean of daily ratios, and the well''s real recent window reads 1066.663410762250 scf/stb. Why are they not the same number?', '["The sweep is built from two day shapes only, while the real window''s ordinary days differ slightly from each other.", "The sweep averages over 7 rows and the real recent window averages over the 30 rows of the baseline definition.", "The sweep is volumetric at that row and the real window figure is a mean of daily ratios, so they answer different questions.", "The real window carries four collapsed days rather than three, which is what pulls its mean of daily ratios below the sweep."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sweep is a demonstration and the window is a result, and quoting the 1.654479651557 ratio of the sweep for the window''s 1.653099061020 swaps one for the other.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 12, 'Between an ordinary teaching recent day and a collapsed one the oil falls by a factor of 12.292682926829 and the gas by a factor of only 4.148936170213. What does that do to the daily gas-oil ratio?', '["It leaves it unchanged, because a gas-oil ratio is formed off one row and both columns on that row fell together.", "It lowers it, since the gas fell as well and the ratio is the gas divided by the oil on the same row.", "It raises it by the factor of 1.350649350649 that the water fell by, since the watercut is what carries the collapse.", "It raises it by a factor of 2.962851782364, from 580.357142857143 scf/stb to 1719.512195121951 scf/stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'derivePoint forms that ratio off the collapsed row alone and windowMean then averages it in at full weight.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 13, 'How does computeKpis arrive at its period watercut and gas-oil ratio?', '["It averages the watercut and the gas-oil ratio that derivePoint left on each point of its window, which is the unweighted mean detectExceptions takes.", "It forms the mean oil, the mean water and the mean gas over its window first, and only then divides those means.", "It reads the volumetric figures buildFieldSeries already stored on each field day and takes the last of them.", "It weights each day''s ratio by that day''s liquid before averaging, which is what makes it a period ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing means is volume weighted by construction, which is why it returns 0.270954648876 as a fraction on the teaching field over 7 days.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 14, 'surveillance_cases.ratioSeam commits neither reading as the expected answer. What does it commit instead, and why?', '["The volumetric reading as expected and the mean of daily ratios as a tolerated variant, since only one of the two can book a volume.", "The mean of daily ratios as expected, because detectExceptions is the only function of the two that prints a severity.", "The measured size of the disagreement, because both readings are live in the shipped studio and resolving it would move numbers a running application prints.", "Nothing numeric at all, since a seam between two defensible readings cannot be gated by a golden."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Publishing a disagreement rather than quietly picking a side is the right thing to have done, and 19.122961825433 per cent is the figure it publishes.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-one-ratio-read-two-ways', 15, 'Why is a mean of daily ratios the better instrument for spotting a well that has changed?', '["It refuses to let a big day drown a few bad ones, so a handful of collapsed days move it while they barely move a ratio of sums.", "It is formed off the window totals, which is what makes it sensitive to a change in the total rather than in the shape.", "It is the only one of the two readings that is compared against a baseline window at all.", "It carries the volume weighting that a surveillance comparison needs before it can name a well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the teaching recent window the mean of daily ratios is 1066.663410762250 scf/stb against 645.250751097758 scf/stb volumetrically, 1.653099061020 times the size.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 1, 'One line in detectExceptions decides which column the whole surveillance list is built on. Which column does it pick for a producer?', '["oil, the calendar volume booked against the date, since the line reads isInjector ? winj : oil and both of those are volumes over a row.", "oilPd, the producing-day rate, because that is the column the module header calls the number saying how the well is performing.", "liquidPd, so that a well whose water has risen is compared on the total fluid it moved rather than on its oil alone.", "Whichever of the two the caller named in the settings, since the rate key is one of the overridable defaults."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The function whose whole job is to say which wells changed reads the one of the two columns that says how much they made.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 2, 'Which of the seven exception checks are built on that rate key?', '["All seven, since every comparison in the function is a recent window mean against a baseline window mean of the same key.", "rate_drop and downtime, because a rate and the hours behind it are the two quantities the key selects between.", "rate_drop, shut_in and injection_drop, and no others.", "rate_drop, gor_rise and watercut_rise, since a ratio is formed off the same column the rate comparison uses."]'::jsonb, 2, 'watercut_rise and gor_rise average ratios derivePoint formed row by row, and downtime averages hoursOn directly, so the hours are in the module and simply not in the rate comparison.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 3, 'The teaching uptime well held its producing-day oil rate at 512.000000000 stb/d on every one of its seven recent days, and the engine reported a rate drop of 38.825312618416 per cent. What did the producing-day rate actually do against the baseline?', '["It fell by 1.856763925729 per cent, a drop far too small to reach the rateDropPct trigger of 20 per cent, which is why the well is reported on the other reading instead.", "It rose by 1.856763925729 per cent, which is what a printed change of -1.856763925729 per cent means on that convention.", "It fell by the same 38.825312618416 per cent, since both readings are formed off the same seven rows of oil.", "It held at exactly zero change, because a rate identical on all seven recent days cannot move against any baseline."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The baseline mean producing-day oil is 502.666666666667 stb/d against a recent 512.000000000000 stb/d, so the two readings of those rows move in opposite directions.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 4, 'On that same well the mean recent hours_on is 14.414285714286 h. What does the downtime check do with it?', '["It raises a medium downtime row, since the hours fell against a baseline mean of 24.000000000000 h and the branch compares a recent mean against that baseline the way the rate check does.", "It raises a high downtime row, because the hours are far enough below a full day to double the threshold.", "It refuses to answer and returns null, since a mean of hours over a window is not itself an hours reading.", "Nothing, because the branch fires only below the downtimeHours threshold of 12 h and 14.414285714286 h is above it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The one exception that would have named uptime as the cause of the reported rate drop sits under its threshold and stays silent.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 5, 'The hours on that well fell 39.940476190 per cent against their baseline, a larger relative move than the 38.825312618416 per cent reported on the oil. Why does no check report it?', '["The hours are compared against their baseline but the result is discarded whenever a rate_drop has already been raised on the same well.", "The hours column is copied through the row rather than computed from it, and only the computed keys are offered to a window comparison, so hoursOn never reaches windowMean at all.", "downtime is a single absolute threshold on the recent mean and is not a baseline comparison at all, so that fall is never computed as a comparison.", "downtime uses the same rateDropPct trigger of 20 per cent, which the hours fall clears, so the row is raised and then sorted out of the list."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well that halves its hours from a full day and still averages above 12 h passes the check, whatever the baseline was.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 6, 'What does an exception row carry, and what does it leave out?', '["It carries a type, a severity, a value and a baseline, and it names neither the column those came from, nor the window length, nor the hours behind them.", "It carries the rate key beside the value, which is how a reader tells a calendar comparison from a producing-day one.", "It carries the recent and baseline window lengths, which differ per well because each well widens its own windows on its own cadence.", "It carries the mean hours_on for the recent window, so that a rate drop can be read against the uptime that produced it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two wells on one field can be compared over different window lengths and nothing in either row says which window was used.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 7, 'The published row booking 500.000000 stb of oil at 12.000000 hours returns oilPd of 1000.000000000 stb/d and liquidPd of 1200.000000000 stb/d. What does that pair show?', '["That liquidPd is formed by adding the oil rate to the water volume rather than to the water rate, which is why it exceeds oilPd by more than the water on the row can account for.", "That the producing-day rate is the calendar volume scaled by the fraction of the field that was on stream that day.", "That a row at half a day is reported at twice its volume in both columns, so the ratio between them is preserved.", "The volume halved against the full row beside it while the rate went up, because the two answer different questions about the same row."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At a full 24 hours the calendar volume and the producing-day rate are the same number, which is the only place they are.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 8, 'A published row books its volumes at hours_on of 0.000000. What does derivePoint return for oilPd, waterPd, gasPd and liquidPd?', '["Zero on all four, which is the reading the field on-count also takes of a row with no hours on it.", "null on all four, because dividing by zero hours would put an Infinity into every window mean downstream.", "The calendar volumes unchanged, which is the fallback the function uses whenever the hours cannot be used as a divisor.", "Infinity on all four, which is what makes a shut-in day visible in the decline series the fitter is handed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is the single most important refusal in the module, because a fabricated record rate would propagate into every mean formed after it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 9, 'A meter reports cumulative hours rather than hours in the day, so a row of 600 stb arrives with hours_on of 26.0. What does the producing-day rate come back as?', '["553.846153846 stb/d, which is below the calendar volume and is a thing a producing-day rate cannot be.", "600.000000000 stb/d, since anything above 24 is clamped back to a full day before the scaling is applied.", "null, because a value outside the nominal 0 to 24 range is refused the way a zero is refused.", "A rate above the calendar volume, since more hours on stream are read as a longer producing period and scale the row up."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing clamps hours_on in surveillance.js, so 48.0 hours gives 300.000000000 stb/d at a ratio of 0.500000000 to the calendar volume.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 10, 'Which function actually consumes oilPd, gasPd and liquidPd?', '["computeKpis, which forms the field mean oil rate off the producing-day column before rolling the ratios up.", "buildFieldSeries, which sums the producing-day rates of every well on a date to get the field rate for that day.", "The decline overlay, through rateSeriesForFit, and nothing else in the file.", "detectExceptions, which reads the producing-day column for producers and the calendar column for injectors."]'::jsonb, 2, 'FIT_STREAMS names the pairing outright, a producing-day key and a calendar key per stream, and only the fit reads the first of the two.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 11, 'The published monthly well files rows of 15000.000000 stb, each covering a month. What does its exception print?', '["A refusal, because a cadence of 30.000000 days leaves too few points inside a widened recent window to compare, so the well is dropped before any window mean is taken on it.", "The same comparison rescaled to a day, since the widening of the windows divides each period volume by the cadence of 30.000000 days it measured off the median gap between points.", "An oil rate in stb/d formed off oilPd, which on those rows is the period volume divided by 30.", "Oil down 20%: 12,000 vs 15,000 stb/d baseline, on a value of 12000.000000000 and a baseline of 15000.000000000 that are means of period volumes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The unit in that sentence is hard-coded and the quantity behind it is a volume over a row, so a month of oil is printed with stb/d after it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 12, 'The teaching monthly well has a seriesCadenceDays of 30.000000, so its windows widen to recentDays 45, baselineDays 120 and staleDays 45. What does the widening not do?', '["Change the number of points inside each window, which is the one thing a cadence measurement cannot influence, since a window is defined in days and the points fall where the ledger put them.", "Rescale the volumes, which are still period totals compared against period totals with a per-day unit printed after them.", "Apply to staleDays, which stays at its default of 7 because a stale check counts days rather than points, so a well filing once a month is called stale seven days after its last row.", "Reach a well whose cadence is 1.0 days, whose windows widen to 45 and 120 in the same run on the same field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The widening fixes the count of points being compared and nothing in the ledger says how long a row covers.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 13, 'The teaching monthly well is reported at high severity on a value of 11600.000000000 against a baseline of 24400.000000000. What was it doing per elapsed day?', '["Producing less by exactly the 52 per cent the message names, since the elapsed span of every row is the same.", "Producing less, 773.333333333 stb/d against 828.571428571 stb/d, which is the fall the high severity is naming.", "Producing more, 828.571428571 stb/d over its last period against 773.333333333 stb/d over the one before.", "Nothing that can be said, because the row carries no elapsed span and the per-day column cannot be formed at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its last row covers fourteen days rather than a month, so the period volume more than halves while the rate rises, and high is the top of the ladder.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 14, 'A ledger arrives with the hours written as the string "12" rather than as a number. What does derivePoint conclude about the uptime?', '["That it is unknown, because the test is Number.isFinite and a string is not a finite number, so the rate falls back to the calendar volume.", "That the well was on for twelve hours, since the string coerces to a number in the division that forms the rate.", "That the row is unusable, so the point is dropped from the series before any window mean is taken.", "That the uptime is zero, which is the same reading the function takes of an absent hours column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a constructed row of 600 stb that fallback returns 600.000000000 stb/d, exactly as null, undefined and NaN do.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-column-that-is-never-read', 15, 'On the teaching uptime well the watercut is 0.180327868852 and the gas-oil ratio 470.000000000 scf/stb on all seven recent days. What follows for the exception list?', '["A watercut_rise is raised anyway, because watercut_rise is formed outside the minOilRate gate and reports on any well.", "A gor_rise is raised, since a gas-oil ratio identical on seven days is compared against a baseline that varied.", "The ratio checks refuse, because a window whose ratios never move has no variance for the window mean to report.", "No ratio check has anything to say about the well, so the rate_drop row is the only thing the list carries on it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every barrel of the reported difference is uptime, and the column that would settle it is on all seven points and is never read.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 1, 'A ledger row arrives with no hours column at all. Which module reads that absence as a full day on stream?', '["surveillance.derivePoint, which substitutes a full day before scaling the volume into a producing-day rate.", "liftScreening.screenLift, whose Number(x) || 0 reads an absent number as the most favourable value the rule has.", "allocation.computeAllocation, whose coercion is Number.isFinite(row.hours_on) ? ... : 24.", "liftAdvisor.num, which takes a fallback per call site and uses a full day wherever an uptime is wanted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The identical column is uptime unknown in surveillance and twenty-four hours on in allocation, and both are called on one ledger by one studio.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 2, 'On a row of 800 stb with a null hours column, surveillance reports an oil producing-day rate of 800.000000000 stb/d and allocation reports an uptime of 1.000000000 and a theoretical oil of 1000.000000000 stb. What is wrong with reading those as consistent?', '["They are the same numbers and not the same claim, because one says the uptime is unknown and the other says the well was on all day.", "Nothing, since both modules resolved the same absence the same way and the numbers agree to the digit.", "The surveillance figure is the one at fault, because a producing-day rate cannot be formed at all without an hours column to divide by.", "The allocation figure is a volume and the surveillance figure is a rate, so the two were never comparable in the first place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only one of the two is entitled to the assertion it makes, and nothing in either return says a value was defaulted.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 3, 'The volumes on one row arrive as numeric strings rather than numbers. Which derived quantities on that row come back exactly right?', '["The liquid and the watercut, because both are formed off columns that were read before the coercion ran.", "None of them, since row.oil_stb || 0 returns the string and every quantity downstream inherits it.", "All of them except the liquid, which is the only member of the return that is not published in the golden.", "The gas-oil ratio at 500.000000000 scf/stb and the oil producing-day rate at 800.000000000 stb/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every quantity formed by multiplication or division coerces its operands back to numbers, and the one formed by addition is liquid, where + on two strings concatenates.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 4, 'On that row the liquid comes back as 800200 stb. What does the damage look like on the two quantities that pass through it?', '["Both are understated by that factor, since a concatenated liquid is larger and a larger denominator lowers whatever divides by it.", "The watercut is understated by a factor of 800.200000000 and the liquid producing-day rate is overstated by the same factor.", "The watercut is understated and the liquid producing-day rate is unaffected, because a producing-day rate is formed by scaling rather than by adding.", "Both are overstated, since the concatenation lengthens the numerator of each of them by the digits of the second volume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The watercut falls to 0.000249937516 and the liquid producing-day rate rises to 800200.000000000 stb/d, one factor in each direction on the same row.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 5, 'Four identical rows of 800 stb of oil on one date reach buildFieldSeries with their volumes as strings. What is the field oil for that date?', '["800800800800.000000000 stb, because the accumulator itself becomes a string on the first row and every later row is concatenated onto it.", "3200.000000000 stb, since d.oil += r.oil_stb || 0 coerces each string back to a number before adding it.", "800.000000000 stb, because a string accumulator cannot take a second value and the later rows are dropped.", "A refusal, since the accumulator is checked against Number.isFinite before the field day is written."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The overstatement factor runs 1.000000000000 on one row, then 500.500000000000, then 333667.000000000000, then 250250250.250000000000, because it grows with the rows sharing a date.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 6, 'One text row alone through buildFieldSeries reports a field oil of 800.000000000 stb and a field water of 200.000000000 stb. What does it report for the field liquid and watercut?', '["A liquid of 1000.000000000 stb and a watercut of 0.200000000000, since the field liquid is formed off the field oil and water totals after they were accumulated.", "A liquid of 800200 stb, the same figure the single row carried, since the field roll-up copies a lone row through unchanged.", "A liquid of 8000200.000000000 stb and a watercut of 0.000024999375, on a wellsOn of 1.", "A refusal on both, because the accumulator is a string and the division that forms a watercut cannot run on one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in the return says a column arrived as text, and the field oil and water beside those two look exactly as a clean pair would.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 7, 'A well produced nothing and filed no ledger row at all for the day. What does computeAllocation credit it with?', '["Nothing at all, since a well with no ledger row on a date is not among the wells that date is split across, the lookup having come back null.", "A full day of uptime, because Number.isFinite(null?.hours_on) is false and the defaultHours of 24 is substituted.", "A zero uptime, which is the same reading it takes of a row that states hours_on as zero.", "The uptime it carried on the previous date, which is the fallback the lookup uses when a date is missing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the demonstration the well that filed nothing takes half the metered oil, at an oil factor of 0.500000000000.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 8, 'Two wells share a meter and only one of them produced. What does the allocation do when the shut well says so with a row at hours_on 0, and what does it do when the shut well files nothing?', '["It gives the producing well every barrel in both cases, since a well with no theoretical volume can take no share whichever way the silence is spelled.", "It refuses the second case outright, because a date on which a well filed no row has no basis on which to allocate.", "It splits the metered volume evenly in both cases, since the uptime enters the theoretical rather than the factor.", "Told the truth it gives the producing well all 1000.000000 stb at an oil factor of 1.000000000000, and told nothing it gives each of them 500.000000 stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The only thing that changed between the two runs is which rows exist, and the second raises three factor_out_of_band diagnostics, one per phase.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 9, 'The silent cases raise factor_out_of_band three times. What does that diagnostic actually say?', '["That the factor left the 0.7 to 1.3 warning band, which is the module noticing a symptom rather than naming a well credited on a default.", "That a well took a share on a date for which it filed no ledger row, which is exactly the condition the demonstration constructs.", "That the facility meter disagrees with the wells own books, which is what an imbalance percentage measures.", "That the test in force on one of the wells has aged past maxTestAgeDays and its theoretical can no longer be trusted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Reading it as a meter problem sends a reader to the facility, and the meter was right in every one of those cases.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 10, 'The teaching field''s silent well takes an allocated share on six dates and none on the fifteen after them. What separates the two stretches?', '["It resumes filing ledger rows on the seventh date, so the default of a full day stops being applied to it.", "Its theoretical oil falls to zero on the seventh date, because a well with no rows accrues no theoretical after its first week.", "The test carrying it ages out, so it has no basis for a share after that even though it is still filing no row.", "The metered volume on those dates is zero, so there is nothing to divide and every well on the field takes nothing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Not one of its allocated days has a ledger row behind it, and its theoretical oil is 688.000000000 stb on each of them.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 11, 'What did detectExceptions say about that same well over the same days?', '["type shut_in, severity high, because a well that stops filing rows has produced nothing in the recent window.", "type downtime, severity medium, since a well with no rows has a mean hours on stream of zero.", "Nothing, because a well with no points in either window is dropped before any comparison is made on it.", "type stale_data, severity medium, value 23.000000000 days against a baseline of 7.000000000 days."]'::jsonb, 3, 'One well, one silence, two modules: surveillance calls it missing and ranks it below every rate drop on the field, and allocation calls it a full day on stream.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 12, 'annualEffectiveDecline is run at Di 0.0015 per day with modelType stated as Hyperbolic and an exponent that came back as NaN. What does it return?', '["null, since an exponent that is not a number is refused the way a non-positive Di is refused.", "42.160601062199 per cent, the published exponential answer at that Di, because !NaN is true and the exponential branch is taken.", "NaN, which is what the hyperbolic form returns once an unusable exponent reaches the exponent of the bracket.", "38.364403131474 per cent, the hyperbolic answer at that Di, because the stated modelType decides the branch before the exponent is read."]'::jsonb, 1, 'null and undefined return the same 42.160601062199 per cent, and the return carries no flag distinguishing that from a b that was genuinely zero.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 13, 'An API is missing from the inputs. What does each of the two lift files make of that absence?', '["Both read it as a 32 degree oil, since num is the shared coercion the two files import from one place.", "screenLift refuses to score a well with no API at all, and liftAdvisor substitutes the 24.6 API the teaching well carries.", "screenLift coerces it to zero, which reads as heavier than any real crude, while liftAdvisor.num(api, 32) reads it as a 32 degree oil.", "Both read it as zero, which is why the ESP loses 20 points and the progressing cavity pump gains 25 in either of them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With the API known the golden scores esp 100 and pcp 55, and with it missing esp 80 and pcp 80, on no information about the well at all.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 14, 'screenLift is handed an empty object and returns the rod pump at 100 with a recommendation. What produced that score?', '["Every missing number defaulted to zero, and a depth of zero against a rate of zero is the most favourable duty the matrix has.", "The matrix starts every method at 100 and the rod pump is the only one of the six carrying no unconditional deduction of its own.", "An empty object is read as the shallow stripper archetype, whose published scores put the rod pump at the top of the list.", "The two sixty-point deductions in the matrix fire on an unstated facility, and both of them happen to fall on other methods."]'::jsonb, 0, 'powerAvailable and gasAvailable are read as inputs?.x !== false, so silence can never trigger those two, and stating both absent returns esp 0 and gasLift 30.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-spellings-of-nothing', 15, 'Which input to annualEffectiveDecline is guarded properly?', '["The exponent, which is checked for a finite value before the three branches are chosen between.", "The modelType, which must be one of Exponential, Harmonic or Hyperbolic before any form is evaluated.", "Neither of the two, since the function returns a number for every input that has ever been handed to it.", "Di, which returns null and refuses at 0, at -0.001, at NaN and at null."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Di of 1e-9 returns 0.000036499990 per cent, which is small and honest, and the guard sits in the same function as the clause that has none.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 1, 'minOilRate defaults to 5. Which comparisons in detectExceptions does it stand in front of?', '["All four of the producer comparisons, since a well too small to be worth reporting should raise nothing at all.", "rate_drop, gor_rise and shut_in, and not watercut_rise, which is formed outside the gate.", "rate_drop and shut_in only, since a ratio check has no rate in it for a minimum rate to be compared against.", "watercut_rise and gor_rise, the two ratio checks, since a ratio formed on a tiny denominator is the unstable one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well too small to have a rate collapse reported can still raise a high watercut exception on the identical rows.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 2, 'A derived well has a baseline window mean of 3.0 and a recent window mean of 1.2 on its calendar oil, and raises exactly one exception. What is it, and what is missing?', '["A high rate_drop, and no watercut_rise, because the watercut check is the one the minimum rate gate covers.", "A medium downtime, and no rate comparison at all, because a well below the minimum rate is compared on its hours instead.", "A high shut_in, since a well falling from 3.0 to 1.2 has effectively stopped and shut_in is raised whatever the size.", "A high watercut_rise, and no rate_drop, although the oil fell 60.000000000 per cent against a trigger of 20 per cent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The message reads Watercut up 51 points: 88% vs 38% baseline, and nothing in that row names the setting that removed the other two.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 3, 'That same well is swept across minOilRate. What does the list look like at 3 and at the default of 5?', '["Three high rows at 3, a rate_drop, a watercut_rise and a gor_rise, and one high watercut_rise at 5.", "One high watercut_rise at 3 and nothing whatever at 5, since raising the gate can only ever shorten a list.", "Three high rows at both, since the gate is compared against the recent mean of 1.2 rather than against the baseline.", "Nothing at 3, because a gate below the baseline mean suppresses every check, and three high rows at 5."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Raising minOilRate does not monotonically reduce a list, because it gates two of the three checks and leaves the watercut check alone.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 4, 'A small well with a baseline of 3.4 stb/d of oil goes to zero volume and zero hours for the whole recent window. What does detectExceptions raise?', '["A high shut_in, which is raised unconditionally and is the one type the severity ladder does not apply to.", "A medium downtime, since a mean of exactly zero hours is the furthest a well can fall below the 12 h threshold the branch is written against.", "Nothing of any kind, because shut_in sits inside the same minimum rate gate and the downtime branch requires the mean hours to be above zero.", "A high rate_drop, since a fall to zero is the largest fall there is and doubles the trigger many times over."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same rows scaled to a baseline of 840 stb/d raise one high shut_in, and the well behaved identically in both runs.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 5, 'The downtime branch reads hrs.mean < s.downtimeHours && hrs.mean > 0. What happens at a mean of 0.10 h and at a mean of 0.00 h?', '["Both raise a medium downtime, since both means sit below the downtimeHours threshold of 12 h and the branch reports every mean that falls under it.", "0.10 h raises a medium and 0.00 h raises a high, because a well at zero hours doubles the threshold crossing.", "Neither raises anything, because a mean below one hour is treated as an unusable reading of the hours column.", "0.10 h raises a medium downtime and 0.00 h raises none, so the worst possible uptime is the one value the check refuses to report."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every row of that sweep also carries a high rate_drop and a high watercut_rise, so the downtime column is the only thing that moves.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 6, 'The gas-oil ratio gate carries the clause base.mean == null || base.mean >= s.minOilRate. When can the first half of it fire?', '["Whenever a window holds rows that made gas and no oil at all, since derivePoint leaves the oil null on such a row while the gas-oil ratio stays finite.", "Never, because base.mean is null only on a window with no points, and in that case gorBase.count is zero and the gate shut two clauses earlier.", "Whenever the baseline window is shorter than the well cadence, so the window mean is taken over a single point and comes back null rather than as a figure.", "Whenever every oil volume in the window arrived as text, since a string mean cannot be formed and comes back null."]'::jsonb, 1, 'derivePoint sets oil with row.oil_stb || 0, so oil is finite on every point that exists, and the probes find 0 of 5 constructions in which the escape hatch could fire.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 7, 'On the teaching field, what does maxTestAgeDays of 0 do?', '["It admits no test at all, so no well takes a share and the whole metered volume is left unallocated.", "It admits only tests dated on the allocated day itself, which on that field is one well at a theoretical oil of 2072.000000000 stb.", "It turns the age check off, so four wells take a share on a theoretical oil of 65033.920833333 stb, more than the default returns.", "It is rejected as a setting, so the default of 180 days is substituted and the run is the default run."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The guard is Number.isFinite(maxTestAgeDays) && maxTestAgeDays > 0, so the strictest-looking value on the dial is the loosest behaviour in the module.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 8, 'A form field for maxTestAgeDays is left blank. What is the difference between the argument being omitted and the argument arriving as null?', '["Omitting it substitutes the default of 180 days and refuses a test 2088 days old, while null skips the guard and accepts that same test.", "There is none, since both spellings are read as absent and both fall back on the default of 180 days before any age is compared.", "Omitting it refuses every test, since no default is applied, while null admits every test on file.", "Omitting it accepts the old test and null refuses it, because null is a finite value and fails the greater-than-zero test."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both are how a form spells not filled in, and they mean opposite things, with nothing in the return naming the limit that chose the tests.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 9, 'On the teaching field the last allocated day carries an oil factor of 1.190463125091 at the default and 0.909283141463 at maxTestAgeDays of 0. What is wrong with tuning the dial by watching the factor?', '["Nothing, since a factor nearer one means the metered total and the theoretical total agree and the tests must therefore be sound.", "The factor is a per cent rather than a ratio, so the two figures cannot be compared against one another directly.", "The factor at 0 is out of the 0.7 to 1.3 warning band, which is the module refusing that setting outright.", "The healthier looking factor is the one produced by the disabled guard, and an allocation factor is dimensionless and carries no evidence about the tests behind it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 0 run also shows the fewest no_test_in_force diagnostics on the sweep at 42, and only factor_out_of_band rises, from none to 9.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 10, 'decimate is asked for at most 1500 points. What does it return off a 1501 point series and off a 3000 point series?', '["1500 and 1500, since the stride is chosen so that the cap is met exactly wherever the series is longer than it.", "751 and 1501, so one call returns half the budget and the other returns one more point than the argument names.", "751 and 1500, since the last point is dropped whenever keeping it would carry the output past the cap.", "1501 and 3000 points, since the stride is 1 in both cases and a series shorter than twice the cap is returned whole."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stride is Math.ceil(n / maxPoints) and is an integer, so the returned count lands wherever the rounding puts it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 11, 'designGasLift refuses a placement for a caller who supplied no facility object at all. Which pressure does the refusal name?', '["900 psig, the fallback inside num(facility?.injectionPsig, 900), and the sentence then advises more surface pressure.", "No pressure at all, since the sentence is written for the case where the caller has not chosen one.", "14.7 psia, the ATM_PSIA constant, which is the only pressure the advisor holds when a facility is absent.", "The wellhead pressure taken off the well record, which is the pressure the injection line is compared against down the tubing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The string high reaches the same sentence because num falls back in silence, and a successful placement carries the same default into its equipment line.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 12, 'runDesignPass reads model.ipr.qmax ?? rateAtPwf(model.ipr, 0) and refuses a target at or above the open flow. Which spelling of an unusable qmax disables the refusal?', '["null, since the fallback then evaluates the inflow at zero pressure and returns a figure large enough to admit any target.", "undefined, since the coalescing operator leaves it in place and an undefined open flow cannot be compared against.", "NaN, because ?? catches only null and undefined, so a NaN open flow passes through and targetRate >= NaN is false.", "Zero, since a target compared against an open flow of zero is below it rather than at or above it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a qmax of NaN the pass runs four design chains against a target of 5000 stb/d, while undefined, null and 0 all refuse and name an open flow of 0 stb/d.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 13, 'The rod loading guard is const loading = d.worstSection ? d.worstSection.loadingPct : NaN followed by if (loading > 100). What does it do with a design whose loading could not be read?', '["It refuses it, since a loading that cannot be read is treated as past the allowable and the rung is thrown out.", "It accepts it as workable, because NaN > 100 is false, and then prints the loading as NaN % of Goodman.", "It skips that rung and moves to the next one on the ladder, which is why six rungs are tried and two are thrown out.", "It throws immediately, which runDesignPass catches and reports as a failure of the design chain."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rung it chose is the one rung whose loading nobody knows, and the two rungs above it were thrown out at 120.0 and 140.0 per cent of their allowable.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 14, 'The guard fires strictly above 100 per cent of the Goodman allowable, and prints an accepted trial to a whole number and a rejected one to one decimal. What do the two sides of the boundary print?', '["An accepted trial prints 99 and a rejected one prints 100.0, so the two verdicts never share a printed figure.", "Both sides print to one decimal, so a reader can always tell 100.0 accepted from 100.0 rejected by the verdict alone.", "A trial at exactly 100.0000000 per cent is thrown out, since the guard is written to fire at the allowable rather than above it.", "Trials at 99.5, 99.9 and 100.0000000 per cent are accepted and print a hundred, and a trial at 100.0000001 per cent is thrown out and prints a hundred as well."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The widest accepted loading printing a hundred is 100.000000 and the widest rejected one is 100.050000, a margin no field measurement could resolve.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-guards-in-the-wrong-place', 15, 'summarizeDeferments is the one function in surveillance.js that can answer differently tomorrow on the same rows. Why is its unanchored day count absent from the teaching material?', '["Because a number that changes on every rebuild cannot be gated, so the finding is stated as a property and as recomputations against a named asOf instead.", "Because the wall clock is read only when an event is still open, and the published case carries no open event to demonstrate it with.", "Because the day count is clamped at one, so the unanchored figure is always one day and carries no information.", "Because the function anchors on the field''s latest ledger date the way every other window in the module does."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Anchored at an asOf of 2024-11-20 that one open event is 173 days, and the count is Math.max(1, endDay - startDay + 1), so an event ending a month before it started is silently one day.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 1, 'The shipped studio passes one rate to both halves of the lift work. What phase does each half take it to be?', '["Both take it as liquid, and the water cut reaches the design chains only as a fluid property for the gravity.", "Both take it as oil, which is why the absolute open flow the pass compares it against is an oil open flow.", "liftScreening takes it as oil because its bands are rate bands, and liftAdvisor takes it as liquid because a pump moves liquid.", "liftScreening documents targetRate as bbl/d of liquid, and liftAdvisor takes the identical field as the oil design rate with the water cut supplied separately."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Neither return carries a field naming the phase it read, and runDesignPass compares the same input against the inflow oil absolute open flow.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 2, 'On the published seam the well makes 300.000000000 bbl/d of oil at a water cut of 70.000000 per cent. What is the liquid rate, and what does the rod pump score on each reading?', '["300.000000000 bbl/d of liquid, since a stated water cut is applied to the gas rather than to the oil, and the rod pump scores 80 either way.", "1000.000000000000 bbl/d of liquid, and the rod pump scores 80 read as oil against 40 read as liquid.", "1000.000000000000 bbl/d of liquid, and the rod pump scores 40 read as oil against 80 read as liquid, since a larger duty is the easier one.", "1000.000000000000 bbl/d of liquid, and the rod pump loses 25 points there, which is the deduction its duty index band carries."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A water cut is a per cent in the two lift modules and a fraction in surveillance and allocation, and forty points is the largest move on that published case.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 3, 'What happens to the recommended set and to the ranked order between those two readings of the published seam?', '["Both change, since a forty point move on one method carries it out of the recommendation band as well as down the order.", "Neither changes, since the deltas fall entirely on methods that were never in the recommendation band to begin with.", "The set is gasLift esp on both readings, and the order changes because the rod pump and the jet pump swap places.", "The set changes to gasLift alone on the liquid reading, and the order is held because the band is applied after the sort."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The scores read as liquid are esp 100 and rodPump 40 against esp 85 and rodPump 80 read as oil, so the leaders are unmoved and the middle of the list is not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 4, 'The teaching handoff passes 310.547186969 bbl/d to the lift half. If that number is the oil rate, what liquid must the method actually move?', '["425.964155029 bbl/d, which is 1.371656781651 times the number handed over.", "310.547186969 bbl/d, since the water was already taken out when the volume was allocated to the well.", "274.331356330 bbl/d, since reading a handed-over liquid as an oil rate takes the water share off it rather than adding one.", "The same figure at a different unit, because bbl/d of liquid and stb/d of oil differ only in the standard conditions."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two answers differ by the water cut of 27.095464888 per cent, which is exactly the thing an allocated oil volume has already been split by.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 5, 'What is the 310.547186969 bbl/d handed to the lift half, before any phase question is asked of it?', '["A producing-day oil rate off the last ledger row of that well, which is already a rate and needs no rescaling.", "The allocated oil for one well on the last allocated day, which is a volume over one calendar day being read as a rate.", "The window mean of the well recent oil, which is the figure the exception row for that well prints as its value.", "The theoretical oil the test in force implies for that well, which is what the allocation divides the metered total against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The water cut of 27.095464888 per cent and the gas-oil ratio of 653.977879649 scf/stb beside it come off the seven day field KPIs, so three different stages meet in one call.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 6, 'Across the teaching sweep the largest single move belongs to plunger lift. Where does it happen and how large is it?', '["At an oil rate of 120.000000 bbl/d it loses 45 points, which is the lowest rate in the sweep and the least favourable for a cycle.", "At an oil rate of 400.000000 bbl/d, where the liquid crosses the ESP band at 500 bbl/d.", "At an oil rate of 650.000000 bbl/d it loses 25 points, which is the largest deduction any method takes anywhere in the sweep.", "At an oil rate of 200.000000 bbl/d it loses 45 points, from 45 read as oil to 0 read as liquid."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The liquid at that row is 274.331356330 bbl/d, which crosses the 200 bbl/d ceiling the plunger rule is written against, and a method at zero is out of consideration entirely.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 7, 'At the lowest rate in the teaching sweep one method gains rather than loses. Which, and what does the gain do?', '["The ESP gains 20 points at an oil rate of 120.000000 bbl/d, and it joins the recommended set on the liquid reading.", "The gas lift gains 10 points there, which is what carries it to 100 and makes it the leader of that row.", "The rod pump gains 15 points there, because the duty index falls below the first of the two bands it is scored against.", "The jet pump gains 20 points there, since the liquid rate carries it over the floor of 100 bbl/d its rule names."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The liquid at that row is 164.598813798 bbl/d, which crosses the ESP band at 150 bbl/d, and no adverse condition may ever raise a score.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 8, 'The rod duty index bands sit at 3 and 6. What are they as rates on the teaching well?', '["150 bbl/d and 500 bbl/d, which are the ESP bands and the only two rate bands the whole matrix is written against.", "3 bbl/d and 6 bbl/d, since the duty index carries the units of the rate it is formed from.", "326.086956522 bbl/d and 652.173913043 bbl/d, since the index is a rate only once the depth of 9200 ft is fixed.", "400 bbl/d and 2000 bbl/d, which are the rod pump reason threshold and the ceiling the progressing cavity pump rule names."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rod pump moves at three separate rates in the sweep as the index crosses those bands, losing 15 points twice and 25 points twice.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 9, 'At an oil rate of 364.000000 bbl/d the sweep shows deltas of zero on all six methods. What does that row establish?', '["That the two readings coincide there, since 364.000000 bbl/d of oil and 499.283068521 bbl/d of liquid are the same duty.", "That a rate can sit between the bands, where the phase reading moves no score and the seam is invisible in the output.", "That the matrix has stopped reading the rate at all by that point, since the depth dominates every rule above 300 bbl/d.", "That the recommendation band is empty on that row, so there are no scores left for the reading to move."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing about the depth, the fluid, the temperature or the facility moves anywhere in that sweep, so a row of zeroes is a rate that happens to sit inside every band on both readings.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 10, 'A screening comes back with a method at 0. What does that zero mean?', '["At or below zero, because the score is clamped, so several differently impossible methods print the same figure.", "A probability of zero that the method will work on this well, since the score is bounded by 0 and 100 the way a probability is.", "That the method was not scored at all, which is how the matrix reports a rule it had no input for.", "Exactly zero, since each deduction is subtracted from 100 and the arithmetic is allowed to run past zero."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A screening score has no unit at all. It ranks methods against each other on one well and it is not a probability of anything.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 11, 'On a well that suits nothing the scores come back jetPump 30, gasLift 5, pcp 5 and three zeroes. What does the return say about the recommendation?', '["It recommends the jet pump at 30, since the band always returns the leader of the ranked list whatever score that leader reached.", "It recommends nothing and raises a diagnostic naming the empty band, which is the field a caller reads to know the list holds no answer.", "It recommends the jet pump and the gas lift, since both are within 15 points of the leader on that list.", "Nothing, because the band is a score at or above the leader less 15 and above 50, so it is empty and no field records that."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The caller receives a ranked list of six with no answer in it, and the jet pump is docked twenty points unconditionally so 80 is its ceiling anywhere.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 12, 'screenLift is handed an empty object and the golden returns rodPump 100, recommended. Why does an empty form score so confidently?', '["Because an empty object is scored against the shallow stripper archetype, whose published leader is the rod pump at 100.", "Because the matrix refuses to deduct on any rule it has no input for, so every method holds the 100 it starts at.", "Missing numbers default to zero, which is the most favourable well the rules have, and missing booleans default to true, which is the most favourable facility.", "Because a rod pump has no rule in the matrix that reads a depth or a rate, so silence about both costs it nothing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rod pump reaches 100 on four printed pros including one saying it is squarely in the rate range beam pumping is most economic at, on a well with no stated depth and no stated rate.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 13, 'powerAvailable and gasAvailable are read as inputs?.x !== false. What follows for the two largest deductions in the matrix?', '["They are the two that silence can never trigger, since an unstated facility is read as fully equipped.", "They fire on any input that does not state the facility, which is why an empty object scores the ESP at 45.", "They are halved when the facility is unstated, so an absent power supply costs the ESP thirty points rather than sixty.", "They are the only two deductions that a missing value can trigger, since every numeric rule needs a number to compare."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Turning powerAvailable false costs the ESP 60 points and turning gasAvailable false costs gas lift 60, and stating both absent on the empty well returns esp 0 and gasLift 30.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 14, 'screeningInputsFromModel is run on one model twice, once with a fluid description and once without. What moves?', '["Nothing moves, since the fluid description supplies only the gas-oil ratio and no rule in the matrix reads it.", "The ESP falls from 100 to 80 and the progressing cavity pump rises from 60 to 80, because an absent API is coerced to zero and zero reads as heavier than any real crude.", "Every score falls, since the seven fields the function returns are cut to five and each missing field costs points.", "The gas lift falls from 100 to 40, since a missing fluid description is read as no gas available on the facility."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function leaves six conditions unstated and every one of them defaults to the favourable reading, so the recommendation narrows from gasLift esp to gasLift alone.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-one-rate-two-phases', 15, 'lift_screening_cases.seams commits the phase disagreement rather than a correct answer. What can the screening oracle gate, and what can it not?', '["It gates the phase, since the seam case supplies both readings and the oracle checks that the liquid one is used.", "It gates neither, since a rules matrix has no independent route and the oracle is a transcription of the module.", "It gates the recommendation band alone, since the scores themselves are policy and only the band is arithmetic.", "It gates what the matrix returns for a given input, walking every rule as a declarative penalty ledger, and it cannot gate which phase the caller meant."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The oracle is a generic scorer with no branch on a method anywhere, which is what makes it independent, and the phase is a question about the caller rather than about the return.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'In what order should the stages of this domain be worked, and why does the order matter?', '["Field roll-ups first, since a field series is what the exception windows are actually taken over on every well.", "The allocation first, because a test in force and an uptime are what the row quantities are later reconciled against, and a facility meter is the only measured number anywhere in the domain.", "Row quantities, then window means, then the allocation, then the lift half, because derivePoint sees one row and everything after it is built on that.", "Whichever order the question asks for, since each of the four stages reads the ledger independently of the others."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An error in a row quantity reaches every later stage silently, and no return anywhere names the stage its input came from.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'A watercut appears in all four modules of this course. What unit does it carry where?', '["A fraction everywhere, since the two lift modules convert on the way in and report on the same basis.", "Points everywhere, since a watercut is only ever reported as a rise against a baseline in this domain.", "A per cent in surveillance and a fraction in the lift modules, which is why a screening rate is quoted in bbl/d.", "A fraction in surveillance and in allocation, and a per cent in the two lift modules."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The teaching handoff carries 27.095464888 per cent into the lift half from a field figure of 0.270954648876 as a fraction, and nothing on either side names the unit.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'A question states a surveillance setting that differs from the default. What should be done with the defaults of recentDays 7, baselineDays 30, rateDropPct 20, watercutRisePts 10, gorRisePct 30, downtimeHours 12, staleDays 7 and minOilRate 5?', '["Use the defaults regardless, since they are what the shipped studio runs and the stated setting is a variation on them.", "Enter the settings the question states and carry none of the defaults in from a worked example.", "Average the stated setting against the default, since the engine reads the settings object over the defaults.", "Use the stated setting for the trigger and the default for the doubling, which the settings object does not carry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A surveillance list is as much a function of its settings as of its ledger, and no returned row names the setting that put it there.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'Grading in this domain is an absolute tolerance in each field''s own units rather than a fraction of anything. What follows for a worked answer?', '["Full precision has to be carried through every step, because a rounded intermediate can fail a field whose method was right.", "Small fields are easier to hit than large ones, since an absolute tolerance is a larger share of a small number and a rounded intermediate costs nothing on one.", "Rounding at each step is safe, since an absolute tolerance is by definition wider than any rounding error.", "A per cent field and a fraction field are graded on the same tolerance, so a fraction should be entered as a per cent."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A watercut is a fraction here and a screening score is unitless, so the same tolerance means different things on different fields.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'What does it mean to say that a quantity in this course does not have one value?', '["It means every quantity is a window mean, so its value depends on the window length and on the cadence that widened it rather than on the function that formed it.", "It means the return objects carry ranges rather than single figures, so a reader picks a value from inside the range and the choice of where in it belongs to the reader.", "It means the engine and its oracle disagree, so a quantity has one value in the module and another in the gate.", "It has a value per function, so a period watercut, a well''s oil and a duty each mean something different depending on which function formed it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A watercut off computeKpis is volumetric and inside detectExceptions is a mean of daily ratios; an oil is the calendar volume or the producing-day rate; a duty is liquid to screenLift and oil to runDesignPass.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'Why is a severity not a measurement?', '["It is a rounded form of the value on the same row, so two rows at one severity carry values within a rounding of each other.", "It is assigned after the sort, so it records where a row landed in the list rather than what the comparison found.", "It names a threshold crossing, and three of the seven types do not use the ladder at all: shut_in is always high, downtime always medium, and stale_data reaches only medium.", "It is a per cent of the trigger the check was run against, which is why a doubling is printed as high."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well silent for 400 days ranks below a forty per cent rate drop and is ordered among the mediums alphabetically.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'The four oracles are genuinely independent routes rather than transcriptions. What do they not gate?', '["Which of two functions a caller should have used, what a missing column means, and what a refusal sentence says.", "What each function returns on the inputs the goldens publish, which is left to the goldens themselves to assert with no independent route behind it.", "The allocation, since a share and a precomputed factor are two routes to one number and cannot be compared.", "The screening matrix, since a rules matrix has no independent formulation for an oracle to walk."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Most of this course lives in what they do not gate, and two seams they do publish are committed as measured disagreements rather than as expected values.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'Two findings in this course are owner decisions rather than defects a lesson can close. Which two?', '["The producing-day column being computed and never read, and the exception message hard-coding stb/d on a period volume, both of them defects inside one file.", "Both readings of a period ratio being live in the shipped studio, and the lift handoff being documented one way and used another.", "The minimum rate gate covering two checks of three, and the age limit turning itself off at zero, since both are guards a caller is free to move.", "The rod loading guard passing a design it cannot read, and the deferment day count reading the wall clock."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Resolving the ratio seam would move numbers a running application prints, which is why the golden commits the size of the gap at 19.122961825433 per cent instead.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'On the published seam the gas-oil ratio rise is 70.033482142857 per cent and 42.737789203085 per cent, and on the teaching well it is 83.907484614181 per cent and 11.250129499613 per cent. What is the difference between the two cases?', '["The published pair is a high against a medium, and the teaching pair is a medium against an info row.", "The published pair straddles the 30 per cent trigger and the teaching pair straddles the 60 per cent doubling.", "The published pair is measured on a mean of daily ratios both ways, and the teaching pair on the volumetric reading both ways.", "The published pair is a high against a medium, and the teaching pair is a high against no exception at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both are the same seam between the same two functions, and the teaching well shows it at a size that removes a well from the list entirely.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'A ledger arrives with one well''s oil volumes as text. Which of that well''s numbers would still reach a rate_drop comparison unharmed?', '["None of them, because the comparison reads the calendar oil column and the row''s producing-day rate, which is the quantity the coercion left correct, is never read by it.", "The producing-day oil rate, since it survives the coercion and is what detectExceptions compares against a baseline.", "The liquid and the watercut, since the coercion damages only the columns that a multiplication touches.", "All of them, since a rate comparison is formed off window means and a mean coerces its operands back to numbers."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A text row leaves the gas-oil ratio and the oil producing-day rate exactly right and the watercut and the liquid producing-day rate wrong by a factor of 800.200000000, and the rate check reads none of the four.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'Two different silences in two different modules each inflate the share a well takes. Name them.', '["An absent hours column read as uptime unknown in surveillance, and a stale_data row that returns early before the other checks run.", "A well filing a row of zeroes at hours_on 24, and a factor that leaves the 0.7 to 1.3 band without being clamped back into it.", "An absent hours column read as a full day in the allocation, and maxTestAgeDays set to zero, which switches the age check off.", "An absent volume coerced to zero by derivePoint, and a decimator returning one point more than the cap its argument names."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At maxTestAgeDays 0 the teaching field returns a theoretical oil of 65033.920833333 stb over four wells against 39172.000000000 stb over two at 60 days.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'One rate crosses from the surveillance and allocation half into the lift half. What has to be said about it before it is used?', '["Which window it was taken over, since a rate handed to a screening is a window mean and the bands are written per window.", "Which phase it is, because screenLift reads it as liquid and runDesignPass reads it as oil, and the teaching handoff is an allocated oil volume over one calendar day.", "Which severity it carried, since a rate that raised an exception is not admissible as a design duty.", "Which of the two decline bases it came off, since a producing-day series and a calendar series fit to different rates."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read as oil the liquid the method must move is 425.964155029 bbl/d against a handed-over 310.547186969 bbl/d, and plunger lift loses 45 points at an oil rate of 200.000000 bbl/d elsewhere in that sweep.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'A hyperbolic fit reports an effective decline of 42.160601062199 per cent. What are the units in that sentence, and what should a reader check?', '["A nominal decline is per day and an effective decline is a per cent over the first year, and the reader should check the exponent, since an unusable one silently takes the exponential branch.", "Both are per cent over a year, and the reader should check the Di, since a Di of zero returns a decline of zero rather than a refusal.", "Both are per day, and the reader should check the model type, since the return names the branch that was taken.", "A nominal decline is a per cent over a year and an effective decline is per day, which is why the fit evaluates the rate law at 365."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published exponential case at a Di of 0.0015 per day returns that same 42.160601062199 per cent, and no flag distinguishes the two.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'Ask what a returned number would look like if the input were missing. What are the three answers this course found?', '["A silence refuses in all three, which is why every module in this course carries a refusal sentence printed word for word in the material.", "A silence returns null in the screening, zero in the allocation, and the default of 180 days in the age guard.", "A silence raises a diagnostic in all three, which is what a reader looks for before trusting a return.", "A silence scores 100 in the screening, takes a full share in the allocation, and turns a limit off in the age guard."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Missing numbers read as the worst possible well and missing booleans as the best possible facility, and none of the returns says a value was defaulted.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'Which of these was certified by this course, as against handed on to another one?', '["Designing a gas lift string, since the refusal sentences and the injection pressure defaults are studied here in detail, down to the equipment line a successful placement prints.", "Reading an effective decline off a fit, since the fit itself is the canonical Arps engine and what this course owns is the series handed to it.", "Sizing an ESP stage and its motor frame, since the reference stage ranges and the headroom rule are part of this domain and are swept in its material.", "Fitting a decline curve, since the fitter and the effective decline are two halves of the same overlay in this module."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four of the six lift methods have a real design chain elsewhere, and what those chains do begins with the rate this course produced, so its phase is the first thing to state.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A surveillance list shows one well twice, once for a fallen oil rate and once for a climbing gas-oil ratio. Which two separate choices decided those two rows?', '["The rate row was taken off the calendar volume rather than the producing-day rate, and the ratio row was formed as a mean of daily ratios rather than volumetrically.", "Both rows were taken off the producing-day column, which is the only column detectExceptions is offered.", "Both were formed volumetrically, which is why they agree with the field KPIs printed beside them.", "The rate row was formed on the window mean and the ratio row on the last point of the window."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two different readings of one set of rows produced two rows on one list, and neither row carries a field naming the reading behind it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'The same NaN is handled by two guards in two files. What do !b in annualEffectiveDecline and loading > 100 in the rod loading check have in common?', '["Both throw on NaN, which runDesignPass catches and reports as a failure of the design chain.", "Both refuse on NaN, which is why an unusable exponent and an unreadable loading are the two documented refusals.", "Both are false for NaN, so each one lets an unusable value through: one silently returns the exponential answer and the other accepts a design as workable.", "Both coerce NaN to zero first, so a missing exponent and a missing loading are read as the most favourable values."]'::jsonb, 2, '42.160601062199 per cent and a rod loading printed as NaN % of Goodman are the two results, and the trusting half of a disagreeing function is the half that is wrong.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'minOilRate removes the rate check and the gas-oil ratio check on a small well and leaves the watercut check standing. Why is that combination worse than it looks?', '["The surviving check is the only one gated on a ratio rather than a rate, so it fires on every well on the field.", "The one check that survives the gate is a check whose value depends on which of two readings formed it, so the only row printed is the one that is ambiguous.", "The surviving check is formed volumetrically, so it disagrees with everything else the list prints on that well.", "The gate is compared against the recent mean rather than the baseline mean, so a falling well escapes it as it falls."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A watercut rise of 21.474523054592 points on one reading and 7.555079897248 on the other is the difference between a high row and no row, and 51 points is what the small well demonstration prints.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'The number handed from the allocation to the lift half is 310.547186969. Name both of the things wrong with using it as a design duty without qualification.', '["It is a producing-day rate rather than a calendar volume, and it was taken over a seven day window rather than one day.", "It is a theoretical rather than an allocated volume, and the screening reads it as oil while the design pass reads it as liquid, on a well whose water cut was never stated anywhere.", "It is a mean of daily ratios rather than a volumetric figure, and it is quoted in stb/d where the screening expects bbl/d.", "It is an allocated volume over one calendar day being read as a rate, and the two lift modules disagree about whether it is oil or liquid."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Read as oil the liquid the method must move is 425.964155029 bbl/d, 1.371656781651 times the figure handed across.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'An API is absent from a data set that reaches both lift modules. What does each of them do, and what does a stated zero do?', '["Both read the absence as 32 degrees, and a stated zero is read as an absence by each of them.", "Both read the absence as zero, and a stated zero is refused as an impossible gravity in each of them.", "screenLift reads the absence as zero and therefore as heavier than any crude, liftAdvisor reads it as 32 degrees, and a stated zero in the advisor is taken literally.", "screenLift refuses to score, and liftAdvisor substitutes the teaching well API of 24.6 degrees."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A stated zero gives a liquid gravity of 1.076045627376, denser than water, while an absent API gives 0.865443425076.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'buildFieldSeries forms the field watercut and gas-oil ratio volumetrically. What happens to that roll-up when one well''s volumes arrive as text?', '["The field liquid is concatenated rather than added, so a single text row reports a liquid of 8000200.000000000 stb and a watercut of 0.000024999375.", "Nothing, since a field roll-up sums the columns and a sum coerces its operands back to numbers before adding them.", "The field oil is wrong and the field liquid is right, since the accumulator holds the oil and derives the liquid at the end.", "The roll-up refuses the date, since a field day whose liquid is not finite is dropped before the ratios are formed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field oil of 800.000000000 stb and the field water of 200.000000000 stb beside those two look exactly as a clean pair would.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Three collapsed days inside a recent window move two different rows on one well''s exception list. Which two, and by which mechanism each?', '["A rate_drop and a downtime, because a collapsed day is a day of lost hours and both checks read the same rows.", "A rate_drop, because the calendar volumes fell, and a gor_rise, because the collapsed days carry a daily ratio of 1719.512195121951 scf/stb into an unweighted mean.", "A watercut_rise and a shut_in, because the collapse is large enough for the well to read as stopped over the window.", "A gor_rise and a stale_data, because the collapsed days are far enough from the field''s latest date to look absent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The teaching seam well carries a rate_drop at 618.142857142857 against 1038.900000000000 beside a gor_rise at 1066.663410762250 against 580.000000000000.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Two dials in two modules are both flattered by silence. What do an unstated screening input and a maxTestAgeDays of zero have in common?', '["Each raises a diagnostic naming the substitution, which is how a reader is warned that a default decided the answer.", "Each refuses to answer, which is why an empty screening and a zero age limit both return an empty result.", "Each is clamped to its nearest legal value, so an unstated input becomes a mid-range one and a zero limit becomes one day.", "Each reads an absence or a strictest-looking setting as the most permissive behaviour, one recommending a rod pump at 100 and the other admitting every test on file."]'::jsonb, 3, 'screenLift({}) returns rodPump 100 recommended, and maxTestAgeDays 0 returns four wells on a theoretical oil of 65033.920833333 stb.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'On the teaching uptime well the downtime check is silent, and on a fully shut small well the downtime check is silent as well. Are those the same silence?', '["Yes, since both wells are below the minimum oil rate and the downtime branch sits inside that same gate.", "Yes, since downtime is always medium and a medium row is suppressed wherever a high row is already raised on the well.", "No: one is a recent mean of 14.414285714286 h sitting above the 12 h threshold, and the other is a mean of exactly 0.00 h, which the branch excludes by requiring the mean to be above zero.", "No: the first is a refusal on an unreadable hours column and the second is a threshold that was not crossed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The worst possible uptime is the one value the downtime check will not report, and a mean of 0.10 h does raise a medium.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'On the published seam the gas-oil ratio rise read one way is 1.638678168636 times the rise read the other. What is that figure?', '["A ratio of two rises, both of them formed on the same rows and neither of them wrong.", "A tolerance the ratioSeam case allows before the two readings are called a disagreement.", "The factor by which the recent gas-oil ratio exceeds its own baseline on the volumetric reading.", "The factor by which a collapsed day is overweighted inside an unweighted mean of daily ratios."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rises are 70.033482142857 per cent and 42.737789203085 per cent, and the golden publishes the overstatement as 19.122961825433 per cent.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'What buys the agreement between the two readings of a period ratio?', '["A short window, since a small number of days leaves the weighting nothing to average over.", "A high rate, since a window of large volumes swamps whatever a low-rate day contributes to it.", "A steady oil rate, since a rate that does not move gives both readings the same denominator.", "Uniformity of the daily ratios across the window, and nothing else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seven collapsed days are a wrecked well and the two readings still match exactly at 1719.512195121951 scf/stb, so smallness is not what does it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A well''s producing-day oil rate is identical on all seven recent days and its calendar oil mean is 307.504761904762 stb against a baseline of 502.666666666667 stb. What is the correct sentence about its performance?', '["It produced very slightly better per hour on stream than it had been, by 1.856763925729 per cent.", "It fell by 38.825312618416 per cent, which is what the exception row correctly reports about the well.", "It fell by 1.856763925729 per cent, a drop too small to reach any trigger in the settings.", "Nothing can be said, since a producing-day rate identical on seven days carries no comparison at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every barrel of the reported difference is uptime, and the two readings of those rows move in opposite directions.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'An exception message reads Oil down 20%: 12,000 vs 15,000 stb/d baseline on a well filing monthly rows. What is wrong with the sentence?', '["The percentage is wrong, since a fall from 15000.000000 stb to 9000.000000 stb over the period is larger than 20 per cent.", "The unit is hard-coded, and the two figures behind it are means of period volumes in stb rather than rates.", "The baseline is wrong, since a monthly well widens its baseline window to 120 days and the mean should cover four rows.", "The severity is wrong, since a fall of that size on a widened window should have been printed as high."]'::jsonb, 1, 'derivePoint reads every row as a calendar day and nothing in a ledger row says how long the row covers.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Which module reads an absent hours column as a full day on stream, and what does the other one do with the identical column?', '["surveillance.derivePoint substitutes 24, while allocation.computeAllocation refuses to allocate the date at all.", "Both substitute 24, which is why a well with no hours column takes a full share and reports a full producing-day rate, the two modules having taken one reading of one absent column.", "allocation.computeAllocation substitutes 24, while surveillance.derivePoint reads it as uptime unknown and leaves the volume unscaled.", "liftScreening substitutes 24 through its Number(x) || 0 coercion, and the other three modules leave the column null."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unscaled volume and a full day happen to be the same number and they are not the same claim, and only one module is entitled to what it asserts.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'A row''s volumes arrive as numeric strings. Which pair of derived quantities is exactly right, and why?', '["The liquid and the watercut, because both are formed before the coercion is applied to the row.", "The watercut and the gas-oil ratio, because a ratio of two strings is computed on their numeric values before either operand is added to anything.", "None of them, because a string in any volume column poisons every quantity derived from that row and every roll-up it reaches afterwards.", "The gas-oil ratio and the oil producing-day rate, because both are formed by multiplication and division only."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two quantities that look right are the two that could not have been wrong, so sanity-checking a row by its ratios proves nothing.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'Four identical rows of 800 stb on one date, with the volumes as text, give a field oil of 800800800800.000000000 stb. What property of that number should be named?', '["It grows with the number of rows sharing a date, because the accumulator itself became a string on the first row.", "It is fixed at that value whatever the row count, since a concatenation is applied once when the column is read.", "It is proportional to the volume on each row, so halving the volumes halves the reported field oil.", "It is the same overstatement the individual rows carry, since the roll-up inherits the row level damage unchanged."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The overstatement factor runs 1.000000000000, then 500.500000000000, then 333667.000000000000, then 250250250.250000000000.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'Which comparisons does the minimum oil rate gate stand in front of, and which one is outside it?', '["It gates all four producer comparisons, so a well below the rate raises nothing at all.", "It gates rate_drop, gor_rise and shut_in, and watercut_rise is formed outside it.", "It gates watercut_rise and gor_rise, the two ratio checks, and leaves the rate comparisons alone.", "It gates nothing directly and is only compared against the recent mean when a row is about to be printed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At a minOilRate of 3 the demonstration well raises three high rows and at the default of 5 it raises one.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'The downtime branch is a mean below the threshold and above zero. Which uptime is the one it will not report?', '["A mean of 12.00 h, which sits exactly on the threshold and is therefore excluded by the strict comparison.", "A mean of 0.10 h, which is below every threshold anyone would set and is treated as an unusable reading.", "A mean of exactly 0.00 h, which is a well that recorded itself shut for the whole window.", "A mean of 24.00 h, since a full day carries no downtime and the branch has nothing to report about it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sweep raises a medium at 0.10 h and nothing at 0.00 h, on rows that otherwise carry a high rate_drop and a high watercut_rise throughout.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'What happens to the teaching field allocation when maxTestAgeDays is tightened from 1 day to 0?', '["The check tightens further, so no test is in force and the whole metered volume goes unallocated.", "Nothing changes, since a value below one day is rounded up to one before the age comparison is made.", "The run refuses, since a zero age limit is not a finite positive number and computeAllocation declines to allocate that date at all.", "The check turns off, so the wells taking a share go from one to four and the theoretical oil rises to 65033.920833333 stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The guard is Number.isFinite(maxTestAgeDays) && maxTestAgeDays > 0, so the strictest-looking value on the dial is the loosest behaviour in the module.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'liftScreening and liftAdvisor receive the same field. What do they take that field to be?', '["liftScreening takes it as a liquid duty in bbl/d and liftAdvisor takes it as the oil design rate, with the water cut supplied to it separately.", "Both take it as a liquid duty, since the design chains are all sized on the fluid a method has to move.", "Both take it as an oil rate, since the absolute open flow it is compared against is an oil open flow.", "liftScreening takes it as an oil rate and liftAdvisor as a liquid duty, which is why the design pass refuses above the open flow."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shipped studio passes one number to both, and neither return carries a field naming the phase it read.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Where in the teaching rate sweep does a method lose the most points to the phase reading alone?', '["The rod pump at an oil rate of 500.000000 bbl/d, which loses 25 points as its duty index crosses a band of its own.", "Plunger lift at an oil rate of 200.000000 bbl/d, which loses 45 points and leaves consideration entirely.", "The ESP at an oil rate of 120.000000 bbl/d, which moves 20 points and changes the recommended set.", "The gas lift at an oil rate of 200.000000 bbl/d, which moves 10 points and takes the lead of the list."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The liquid at that row is 274.331356330 bbl/d, past the 200 bbl/d ceiling the plunger rule is written against.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'A screening returns six ranked methods and recommends none of them. What in the return says so?', '["The leader''s score, which is set to zero whenever no method clears the band.", "A diagnostic naming the empty band, which is the only diagnostic the screening return carries, printed beside the ranked list wherever the leader fails to clear fifty.", "Nothing, because the band is a score at or above the leader less 15 and above 50, and no field records that it came back empty.", "The recommended list, which carries the leader anyway so that a caller always has an answer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a well suiting nothing the scores are jetPump 30, gasLift 5, pcp 5 and three zeroes, and the caller gets a ranked list with no answer in it.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Both severity columns of the published seam are committed by the golden. What do they show?', '["A high on both readings of the gas-oil ratio and a medium on both readings of the watercut.", "A medium on the mean of daily ratios and a high on the volumetric reading, since a ratio of sums is the larger.", "An info row on the volumetric reading, since a rise that clears no trigger is still ranked at the bottom of the ladder of three severities.", "A high on the mean of daily ratios against a medium on the volumetric reading, on both the gas-oil ratio and the watercut."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rises are 70.033482142857 against 42.737789203085 per cent, and 20.938677629325 against 18.603480205160 points.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'In the teaching sweep of collapsed days, where is the disagreement between the readings at its widest?', '["At 5 of 7 collapsed, where the mean of daily ratios is 1.803701012112 times the volumetric figure.", "At 7 of 7 collapsed, since the collapse is total and the well is at its worst there.", "At 1 of 7 collapsed, since a single low-rate day speaks for a seventh of the window and is least diluted.", "At 4 of 7 collapsed, where the watercut difference of 0.166155176525 is the largest in the sweep."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The disagreement is a mixture effect, so it closes at both ends of the sweep and peaks in the middle.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'A row of 600 stb arrives with hours_on of 26.0. What does surveillance return and why is it not refused?', '["null, because a value outside the nominal range is refused exactly as a zero is refused.", "553.846153846 stb/d, because nothing in surveillance.js clamps the hours column to a nominal 24.", "600.000000000 stb/d, because a value above 24 is read as uptime unknown and the volume is left unscaled.", "612.765957447 stb/d, because the extra hours are added to the producing period and lift the rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That figure is below the calendar volume, which is a thing a producing-day rate cannot be, and at 168.0 hours the same row returns 85.714285714 stb/d.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Which reader consumes the producing-day columns, and how often are those columns computed?', '["Only computeKpis reads them, and they are computed on rows that carry a finite hours column.", "detectExceptions reads them for producers and buildFieldSeries for the field, and they are computed on every row.", "Only the decline overlay reads them, and they are computed on every row derivePoint touches.", "Nothing reads them, and they are computed only when a caller asks for a decline series."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A column existing is not a column being read, and FIT_STREAMS names the producing-day key and the calendar key of each stream side by side.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Two wells share a meter and one of them files no ledger row at all. What does the allocation give the well that produced everything?', '["All of it, since a well with no ledger row has no uptime and therefore no theoretical volume to take a share on that date, the lookup having found no hours to read.", "All of it, with a diagnostic naming the well that filed nothing, which is what factor_out_of_band reports.", "Nothing at all, since a date on which any well is missing a row has no basis and is left out of the allocation.", "Half the metered oil, at an oil factor of 0.500000000000, because the silent well is credited with a full day and a theoretical of its own."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Told the truth about the shut well with a row at hours_on 0, the engine gives the producer all 1000.000000 stb at a factor of 1.000000000000.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'One well''s silence is read by two modules on the same days. What does each conclude?', '["detectExceptions raises a medium stale_data at 23.000000000 days against a baseline of 7.000000000 days, and computeAllocation credits the well with a full day on stream.", "Both read it as missing, which is why the well takes no share and raises a stale_data row at the same time.", "Both read it as a full day, which is why the well takes a share and raises nothing on the exception list.", "detectExceptions raises a high shut_in and computeAllocation leaves the well out of the split entirely."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stale_data row also ranks below every rate drop on the field, because that type cannot pass medium however long the silence.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'annualEffectiveDecline is asked for a hyperbolic answer and the exponent arrives as null. What comes back, and what is dangerous about it?', '["null, which is safe, since the same guard that refuses a non-positive Di refuses an unusable exponent.", "42.160601062199 per cent, which is the published exponential answer at that Di and carries no flag saying the branch was taken on a falsy exponent.", "NaN, which is visible, since an unusable exponent propagates through the bracket and out of the return.", "38.364403131474 per cent, the hyperbolic answer, since the stated model type decides the branch before the exponent is read."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A string exponent of 0.5 works and returns 38.364403131474 per cent, so the broken input gives a plausible number and the sloppy one gives the right answer.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'The gas-oil ratio gate carries a clause meant to let a well through when its baseline oil is unknown. What is its status?', '["It fires on any window whose rows made gas and no oil, which is the case the clause was written for, since such a row carries a gas-oil ratio and no oil mean.", "It fires whenever the baseline window holds fewer points than the cadence widening asks for.", "It is unreachable, because oil is finite on every point that exists and a window with no points has already failed an earlier clause.", "It fires on a window whose oil column arrived as text, since a string mean comes back as null."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Five probes of forty identical rows each find 0 of 5 constructions in which the escape hatch could fire, which proves it by construction rather than by argument.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'A planner pastes a gas lift refusal into a report. Which number in the sentence did the caller never supply?', '["The flowing gradient, which is computed from a well model the caller never handed in.", "The design rate, which the refusal advises lightening and which is taken from the screening rather than the pass.", "None of them, since a refusal is only ever printed on inputs the caller stated.", "The 900 psig it names as the injection pressure, which is the fallback inside num(facility?.injectionPsig, 900)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sentence then advises more surface pressure, and a successful placement carries the same defaulted pressure into its equipment line.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'The rod loading guard fires strictly above 100 per cent of the allowable and prints at two precisions. What is the width of the collision?', '["A trial at exactly 100.000000 per cent is accepted and prints a hundred, and one at 100.0000001 per cent is thrown out and prints a hundred as well.", "A trial at 99.4000000 per cent and one at 100.3000000 per cent both print a hundred despite opposite verdicts.", "Every trial between 99.5000000 and 101.0000000 per cent prints a hundred, whichever side of the guard it falls.", "Only a trial at exactly 100.0000000 per cent can print a hundred, and it is the one value the guard refuses."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The widest accepted loading printing a hundred is 100.000000 and the widest rejected one printing 100.0 is 100.050000, a margin no field measurement resolves.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'runDesignPass refuses a target at or above the inflow absolute open flow. Which spelling of the open flow disables the refusal, and what runs instead?', '["Zero, because a target compared against zero is above it and the comparison is written the other way round, which is why a zero open flow admits every target.", "NaN, because the coalescing operator catches only null and undefined, so four design chains run against a target of 5000 stb/d.", "undefined, because the fallback then evaluates the inflow at zero pressure and returns a figure no target can reach at any rate.", "A negative open flow, because the comparison is made on the absolute value of the figure supplied."]'::jsonb, 1, 'undefined, null and 0 all refuse and name an open flow of 0 stb/d, which is the absence of an inflow model rather than a well that cannot flow.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'The rod duty index bands of 3 and 6 become rates once a depth is fixed. What are they at the teaching well?', '["50 bbl/d and 2000 bbl/d, which are the floor and the ceiling the progressing cavity rule names.", "150 bbl/d and 500 bbl/d, which are the two bands the ESP rules are written against.", "326.086956522 bbl/d and 652.173913043 bbl/d at 9200 ft.", "3 bbl/d and 6 bbl/d, since a duty index carries the unit of the rate it was formed from."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The index on the teaching well is 2.857034120116 on the oil rate and 3.918870226267 on the liquid rate, so one reading crosses a band the other does not.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'What does a screening score of 100 mean, and what does a score of 0 mean?', '["100 means the method is certain to work and 0 means it is certain not to, which is why the band is set at 50, and a method between the two is ranked by how far it fell.", "100 is the ceiling any method can reach and 0 is the floor, so both are the ends of a probability scale.", "100 means every rule was satisfied and 0 means no rule could be evaluated, which is how silence is reported, so an empty input scores every method at zero.", "100 is where every method starts and means no rule found a reason to deduct, and 0 means at or below zero because the score is clamped."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The score has no unit, ranks methods against each other on one well, and survives wherever the matrix had no reason to deduct, including every reason it was never told.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'The four oracles take genuinely independent routes. Where does most of this course live in relation to them?', '["In what they do not gate: which of two functions a caller should have used, what a missing column means, and what a refusal sentence says.", "In what they gate most tightly, since a returned number is the only thing a course can examine, and the four goldens publish every input a question could be asked on.", "In the two seams they publish, which are the only findings the course is entitled to teach.", "In the difference between the module and the oracle, since a disagreement between the two is a defect by definition, and this course catalogues those."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two seams they do publish are committed as measured disagreements rather than as expected values, which is the right thing to have done.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'In what order do the stages of a surveillance and allocation problem have to be worked?', '["The lift half first, since the duty it needs decides which of the earlier stages have to be run at all.", "Row quantities first, then the window means, then the allocation, then the lift half.", "The window means first, since a row quantity has no meaning until the window it belongs to is defined.", "The allocation first, since the metered total is the only measured number and everything else is derived from it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'derivePoint sees one row and nothing else, and an error in a row quantity reaches every later stage silently.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'A surveillance list is handed over with no settings recorded beside it. What can be concluded from it?', '["That the defaults were used, since the settings object is optional and the module substitutes its documented defaults whenever a caller passes none.", "That every well on the field was compared, since a settings change can only alter a severity and not a membership, which the ledger fixes.", "Very little, because the list is as much a function of its settings as of its ledger and no row names the setting that put it there.", "That the severities are comparable across wells, since a severity is a threshold crossing rather than a size."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Raising minOilRate does not even shorten a list monotonically, because it gates two of the three checks it touches.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'What is the single habit that most of this course''s findings come back to?', '["Recomputing the number by hand, because a returned figure cannot be trusted until it has been reproduced against the oracle that gated it.", "Reading the severity first, because a severity ranks the rows and tells a reader where to start.", "Checking the units, because every disagreement in this course turns out to be a unit conversion that was missed, between a fraction and a per cent.", "Naming the function beside the number, because almost every finding here is a disagreement between two functions in one file."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A lesson that says the engine instead of naming which of two functions has not made the point.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Which of these is the safest reading of an exception at high severity?', '["That some threshold was crossed twice over on one reading of one comparison, and nothing about how large the underlying change is.", "That the change is at least twice the trigger, so the well has deteriorated at least twice as far as a medium well has on the same comparison.", "That the well is among the worst on the field, since the list is sorted by severity and then by size within it, which is what put it at the top.", "That the comparison was made on more data than a medium row, since a high needs both windows to be full."]'::jsonb, 0, 'shut_in is high whatever its size, and the sort ties break alphabetically on the well name, so reading order carries no information about size.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'What does a stale_data row rank below, and why can it never rank above it?', '["Nothing, since a well that has stopped reporting is the most urgent thing a list can carry.", "Every high severity row on the field, because a doubled stale_data reaches medium and no further.", "Only a shut_in row, since shut_in is the one type that is high unconditionally.", "Every row raised on a well with more recent data, since the sort is by the age of the last point on the field ledger."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A well silent for 400 days sits among the mediums and is ordered alphabetically there, and the check also returns early so no other comparison on that well is made.'),
  ('surveillance', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Where does a course number that is not in the digest come from, and what should be done with it?', '["It comes from the goldens and may be used wherever the golden case is named beside it.", "It comes from the engine re-run and may be used once it has been checked against the oracle, which is the route the digest itself took.", "It has no provenance, and it may not be used, because recomputing a figure is not a substitute for a published or derived one.", "It comes from the teaching field and may be used as long as the field is described as invented and the number is labelled as a teaching one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'golden, derived and teaching are three different kinds of claim, and a reader is entitled to know which is in front of them.');
